FEATURE: resizer-fixes
DATE: 2026-08-27
SCOPE: repair the Resizer module end to end and load its stylesheet

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
AFFECTED PROJECTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

vUX library (this repository) — the only project affected. No public API was
removed or renamed, so no consumer change is required. Two additions
(resizerObj.destroy(), and the left/top edge inversion) and one previously
inert option (config.myResizeHandler) become usable.

  - vUX-resizer.js   11 fixes; see logs/vUX-resizer.js_ai_change.log
  - ChangeLog.md     four bullets under v4.0.0-beta Fixed, one under Added

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STARTING POSITION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The module had not been touched since 2024-12-31 and did not work. It was not
degraded or partially working — a mousedown on a handler threw before it set any
state, so the component never responded to a drag at all. Three independent
ReferenceErrors, a drag formula that cancelled to an identity, and a stylesheet
that was never loaded meant nothing about the component functioned: not the
resize, not the callback, not the highlight, not the appearance of the handlers.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT WAS WRONG
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Crashes (module code is strict mode, so these threw rather than creating globals):

  1. `callBack` was read and assigned but never declared. Setting
     config.callBack threw immediately; even leaving it unset, resize() threw on
     every mousemove of every drag.
  2. pinPoint() assigned to `targetElementleWidth`/`targetElementleHeight` —
     misspellings of the module's own variables. This threw on the function's
     first statement, so no direction was resolved and `pin` was never set. This
     is the defect that made the component inert.
  3. The resizeHandlerProperties setter read `targetConfig[0].length` (the length
     of an entry pair, always 2) where it meant the key count, so its guard never
     fired, and passing {} threw a bare TypeError instead of the intended error.

Wrong behaviour:

  4. `targetElementWidth + (e.clientX - targetElementWidth)` reduces to
     `e.clientX`. The element was sized to the pointer's distance from the
     viewport edge, so it snapped to the cursor, discarded the grab offset, and
     was skewed by its own page offset. Identical defect on the Y axis.
  5. Nothing inverted the delta for a handler on the left or top edge, so those
     two position settings produced controls that shrank the element when
     dragged outward.
  6. unPin() cleared "on" from `$$.ss(".resizeHandle")` — the first handler in
     the document, not the one dragged. Both ends used toggle() rather than
     add/remove, so the class tracked drag-count parity rather than drag state.
  7. `if(yhandler != "")` tested an element against a string where it meant
     yStyle, and both axes emitted rules against the bare `.resizeHandle`
     selector, so x and y styles overwrote each other and an empty y style could
     blank a configured x style.
  8. resizeHandlerProperties.styles.both was validated but read by nothing.
  9. config.myResizeHandler was validated and stored but never used — binding was
     hardcoded to "resizeHandle", so supplying a custom handler produced a
     component with nothing listening to it.
 10. assets/css/resizer.css was never linked. Handlers had no position, no size
     and no cursor: invisible and ungrabbable out of the box.
 11. Unknown or miscased resizeHandlerProperties keys were silently ignored
     (no final else, and a discarded-result comparison where the code meant to
     lowercase the key). The "styles" branch named `position` in every error it
     raised, having been copied from that branch.
 12. Every listener was permanent and the injected handlers had no removal path,
     so there was no way to tear a Resizer down.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT CHANGED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Resizing now uses the standard drag model: mousedown records the pointer position
and the element's size, and each mousemove sets size = startSize + distance
travelled. The grab offset cancels out of the subtraction, so where inside the
handler the drag starts no longer matters and the element's page position no
longer enters the arithmetic. A handler on the left or top edge inverts the
distance, which is what makes position:{x:"left"} and position:{y:"top"} behave
as their names suggest.

Handler identification moved out of $$.attachEventHandler() into named listeners
on document plus a closest()-based lookup. That does three things at once: it
lets destroy() unregister what initialize() registered, it accepts a mousedown
landing on a child of the handler (which matters for consumer handlers containing
a grip or an icon), and it lets the axis fall back to the configured direction
when the handler carries no marker class — which is what finally makes
config.myResizeHandler usable.

The stylesheet is linked through the same processAssetPath()/linkStyleSheet()
shape used by ModalDisplayer and FormComponents, so it resolves against
vModel.core.data.assetPath and honours the data-library-root contract.

Generated handler CSS is scoped per axis, styles.both fills in for an axis with
no style of its own, and initialize() is now idempotent — it previously appended
a duplicate handler, listener set and <style> element on every extra call.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEW API
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  resizerObj.destroy()   removes the module's listeners and any handlers it
                         injected, and releases a drag in progress. Additive;
                         nothing existing calls it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
VERIFICATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

There is no test runner in this repository and no example page for Resizer, so
this is static verification plus an extracted-arithmetic check:

  - The module parses as an ES module with the core import stubbed.
  - The new delta arithmetic was lifted verbatim into a standalone script and run
    over 8 cases — right-edge grow/shrink, left-edge grow/shrink, max clamp, min
    clamp, the negative guard, and a zero-distance drag. All 8 pass. The old
    expression returns the raw pointer coordinate in all 8.
  - The fixed file was grepped for each original defect signature; none survives.

Not verified: real drag behaviour in a browser, and that resizer.css resolves at
runtime under a live data-library-root.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FOLLOW-UP
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Resizer has no page under examples/ and no guide under doc/, both of which
CLAUDE.md requires of every module. An examples/resizer/ page would also convert
the two unverified items above into something demonstrable, and is the natural
next piece of work on this module.
